  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{\u2032}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{\u2032}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
